# Apache Flink Streaming Session Windows

## Overview
Apache Flink provides session windows for grouping events that are close together in time. Session windows are useful when you need to detect periods of activity separated by gaps of inactivity.

## Key Concepts

### Session Windows
- Session windows group events by a **session gap** - a period of inactivity
- Unlike tumbling or sliding windows, session windows don't have a fixed size
- A session ends when no new events arrive within the gap duration
- Each key can have different session boundaries

### Event Time vs Processing Time
- **Event time**: timestamps embedded in the data (preferred for deterministic results)
- **Processing time**: wall clock time when the event is processed
- For batch-like processing of historical data, event time with watermarks is essential

## API Usage (Flink 1.18.0)

### Setting Up Event Time

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
// Event time is the default in Flink 1.18
```

### Assigning Timestamps and Watermarks

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.SerializableTimestampAssigner;
import java.time.Duration;

DataStream<MyEvent> stream = source
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy.<MyEvent>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
            .withTimestampAssigner((event, timestamp) -> event.getTimestamp())
    );
```

### Session Windows with Event Time

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.EventTimeSessionWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

stream
    .keyBy(event -> event.getKey())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new MyAggregateFunction());
```

### ProcessWindowFunction for Session Windows

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.windowing.ProcessWindowFunction;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.windows.TimeWindow;
import org.apache.flink.util.Collector;

public class SessionCounter extends ProcessWindowFunction<TaskEvent, Tuple2<Long, Integer>, Long, TimeWindow> {
    @Override
    public void process(Long key, Context context, Iterable<TaskEvent> elements, Collector<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> out) {
        int count = 0;
        for (TaskEvent e : elements) {
            count++;
        }
        out.collect(new Tuple2<>(key, count));
    }
}
```

### Aggregate + ProcessWindowFunction (Efficient)

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.functions.AggregateFunction;

public class CountAgg implements AggregateFunction<TaskEvent, Integer, Integer> {
    @Override public Integer createAccumulator() { return 0; }
    @Override public Integer add(TaskEvent value, Integer acc) { return acc + 1; }
    @Override public Integer getResult(Integer acc) { return acc; }
    @Override public Integer merge(Integer a, Integer b) { return a + b; }
}

// Usage: combine aggregate with process function
stream
    .keyBy(event -> event.getJobId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new CountAgg(), new MyProcessWindowFunction());
```

## Important Notes

- **Watermarks**: For the session gap to work correctly, watermarks must advance past the session gap after the last event in a session. Use `forBoundedOutOfOrderness` or `forMonotonousTimestamps`.
- **Late events**: Events arriving after the watermark has passed their window will be dropped by default. Configure allowed lateness if needed.
- **Memory**: Session windows buffer all events in the window. Use aggregate functions to reduce memory pressure.
- **Timestamps in milliseconds**: Flink uses millisecond timestamps internally. If your data uses microseconds, convert by dividing by 1000.

# Reading Gzipped CSV Files in Apache Flink

## Overview
Apache Flink can read compressed (gzipped) CSV files as data sources using various approaches. For simple CSV parsing in a streaming context, a custom SourceFunction or using `readTextFile` with decompression is common.

## Approaches

### 1. Using readTextFile (Simplest for Batch-like Processing)

```java
// readTextFile automatically handles .gz decompression
DataStream<String> lines = env.readTextFile(filePath);

DataStream<MyEvent> events = lines
    .map(line -> MyEvent.fromCsv(line))
    .filter(event -> event != null); // filter out malformed lines
```

**Note**: `readTextFile` is a bounded source - it reads the file and finishes. This works well for batch processing of historical data files. It automatically detects `.gz` extension and decompresses.

### 2. Custom SourceFunction

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.source.SourceFunction;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.zip.GZIPInputStream;

public class GzipCsvSource implements SourceFunction<String> {
    private final String filePath;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public GzipCsvSource(String filePath) {
        this.filePath = filePath;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(SourceContext<String> ctx) throws Exception {
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(new GZIPInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))))) {
            String line;
            while (running && (line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                ctx.collect(line);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel() {
        running = false;
    }
}
```

### 3. Parsing CSV Lines

```java
public class TaskEvent implements Serializable {
    public long timestamp;
    public String missingInfo;
    public long jobId;
    public int taskIndex;
    public long machineId;
    public int eventType;
    // ... more fields

    public static TaskEvent fromCsv(String line) {
        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);  // -1 to keep trailing empties
        TaskEvent event = new TaskEvent();
        event.timestamp = fields[0].isEmpty() ? 0 : Long.parseLong(fields[0]);
        event.missingInfo = fields[1];
        event.jobId = fields[2].isEmpty() ? 0 : Long.parseLong(fields[2]);
        event.taskIndex = fields[3].isEmpty() ? 0 : Integer.parseInt(fields[3]);
        // ... parse remaining fields, handle empty fields
        return event;
    }
}
```

## Key Considerations

- **Empty fields**: The Google cluster trace CSVs have empty fields (consecutive commas). Always use `split(",", -1)` and check for empty strings before parsing.
- **Timestamps**: The dataset uses microseconds. Flink uses milliseconds for event time. Divide by 1000 when assigning timestamps.
- **Large numeric IDs**: Job IDs and machine IDs can be large numbers - use `long` type.
- **Serialization**: All data types used in Flink streams must implement `Serializable`.

# Flink Keyed Streams, Joins, and Aggregation Patterns

## Overview
This skill covers patterns for working with keyed streams in Flink, including joining two streams, grouping by key, and computing aggregations across windows.

## KeyBy and Keyed Streams

```java
// Key by a field
DataStream<TaskEvent> taskStream = ...;
KeyedStream<TaskEvent, Long> keyedByJob = taskStream.keyBy(event -> event.jobId);
```

## Connecting Two Streams (CoProcessFunction)

When you need to combine information from two different streams (e.g., task events and job events):

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.co.CoProcessFunction;

taskStream
    .connect(jobStream)
    .keyBy(task -> task.jobId, job -> job.jobId)
    .process(new CoProcessFunction<TaskEvent, JobEvent, Result>() {
        // State for buffering
        private ValueState<...> state;

        @Override
        public void processElement1(TaskEvent task, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
            // Process task event
        }

        @Override
        public void processElement2(JobEvent job, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
            // Process job event - e.g., when job finishes, emit result
        }
    });
```

## Finding Maximum Across Windows

To find the session/window with the most events per key:

### Approach 1: Session Window + Reduce/Aggregate, then Global Window Max

```java
// Step 1: Count events per session per key
DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> sessionCounts = taskStream
    .keyBy(e -> e.jobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new CountAgg(), new AddKeyProcessFunction());

// Step 2: Find max count per key using a reduce
DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> maxPerKey = sessionCounts
    .keyBy(t -> t.f0)
    .reduce((a, b) -> a.f1 >= b.f1 ? a : b);
```

### Approach 2: Using KeyedProcessFunction with State

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.KeyedProcessFunction;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueState;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueStateDescriptor;

public class MaxSessionFinder extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Tuple2<Long, Integer>, Tuple2<Long, Integer>> {
    private ValueState<Integer> maxCount;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        maxCount = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("maxCount", Integer.class));
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Tuple2<Long, Integer> value, Context ctx, Collector<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> out) throws Exception {
        Integer current = maxCount.value();
        if (current == null || value.f1 > current) {
            maxCount.update(value.f1);
            // Emit updated max (will produce intermediate results)
            out.collect(value);
        }
    }
}
```

## Writing Output to Files

```java
import org.apache.flink.core.fs.FileSystem;

stream.writeAsText(outputPath, FileSystem.WriteMode.OVERWRITE)
      .setParallelism(1);  // Single file output

// Or use addSink
stream.addSink(new StreamingFileSink<>(...));
```

## Tuple Output Format

To produce `(jobId,count)` format:

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.java.tuple.Tuple2;

// Tuple2 toString produces: (field1,field2)
Tuple2<Long, Integer> result = new Tuple2<>(jobId, count);
// result.toString() -> "(12345,42)"
```

## Important: Bounded Stream Considerations

When processing a finite/bounded data source (like a file):
- All windows will fire when the source finishes and the final watermark (Long.MAX_VALUE) is emitted
- Use `reduce()` after session windows - for bounded streams, the reduce will emit final results when the stream ends
- `writeAsText` with parallelism 1 ensures a single output file

# Flink Output Formatting and File Sinks

## Overview
This skill covers how to format output data and write it to files in Apache Flink streaming jobs.

## Writing to Text Files

### Using writeAsText (Deprecated but functional in Flink 1.18)

```java
import org.apache.flink.core.fs.FileSystem.WriteMode;

resultStream
    .writeAsText(outputPath, WriteMode.OVERWRITE)
    .setParallelism(1);  // Ensures single output file
```

This uses `toString()` on each element. For `Tuple2<Long, Integer>`, the default toString produces: `(12345,42)`.

### Using writeAsCsv

```java
resultStream
    .writeAsCsv(outputPath, WriteMode.OVERWRITE)
    .setParallelism(1);
```

### Using Custom Sink

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.sink.SinkFunction;

resultStream.addSink(new SinkFunction<Tuple2<Long, Integer>>() {
    @Override
    public void invoke(Tuple2<Long, Integer> value, Context context) {
        // Custom write logic
    }
});
```

### Using map() for Custom Formatting

```java
resultStream
    .map(t -> "(" + t.f0 + "," + t.f1 + ")")
    .writeAsText(outputPath, WriteMode.OVERWRITE)
    .setParallelism(1);
```

## Output Format Requirements

For the format `(first_int,second_int)`:
- Flink's `Tuple2.toString()` already produces this format: `(value1,value2)`
- No custom formatting needed when using `Tuple2<Long, Integer>`

## Using AppBase.sinkOrTest Pattern

```java
// For testability, use the AppBase pattern:
import clusterdata.utils.AppBase;

resultStream.addSink((SinkFunction<Tuple2<Long, Integer>>) AppBase.sinkOrTest(
    new org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.sink.filesystem.StreamingFileSink<>(...)
));

// Or simpler with writeAsText and sinkOrTest fallback
if (outputPath != null) {
    resultStream.writeAsText(outputPath, WriteMode.OVERWRITE).setParallelism(1);
} else {
    resultStream.addSink((SinkFunction) AppBase.sinkOrTest(null));
}
```

